From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rudi Heitbaum <rudi@heitbaum.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] DEBUG: ASoC: rt5645: re-assert the DMIC1 data pin mux

NOT FOR SUBMISSION - a probe for one hypothesis, drop once answered.

The Coral's DMIC data line is GPIO5, shared with the I2S2 DAC pin. The
GPIO5 case in rt5645_i2c_probe() writes three things; reading the hardware
back shows two of them took and GPIO_CTRL1 bit 4, I2S2_DAC_PIN_GPIO, did
not, so the pin never leaves I2S2 mode and cannot carry DMIC data. The
register cache does not explain it - the default table has
{ 0xc0, 0x0000 }, so regmap_update_bits() would have written it.

Re-assert the bit at the end of the component probe and see whether the
microphones start working.
---
 sound/soc/codecs/rt5645.c | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/sound/soc/codecs/rt5645.c b/sound/soc/codecs/rt5645.c
index e7e39671a576..70f0d5abec5e 100644
--- a/sound/soc/codecs/rt5645.c
+++ b/sound/soc/codecs/rt5645.c
@@ -3500,6 +3500,19 @@ static int rt5645_probe(struct snd_soc_component *component)
 	if (!rt5645->pdata.jd_mode && rt5645->gpiod_hp_det)
 		rt5645_irq(0, rt5645);
 
+	/*
+	 * DEBUG, NOT FOR SUBMISSION. The Coral's DMIC data line is GPIO5,
+	 * shared with the I2S2 DAC pin. Reading the hardware back shows
+	 * GPIO_CTRL1 bit 4 clear even though the GPIO5 case in
+	 * rt5645_i2c_probe() writes it, which would leave the pin in I2S2
+	 * mode and unable to carry DMIC data. Re-assert it and see whether
+	 * the microphones start working. Check with:
+	 *   grep ^0c0: /sys/kernel/debug/regmap/2-001a-nocache/registers
+	 * which should read 4090 rather than 4080.
+	 */
+	regmap_update_bits(rt5645->regmap, RT5645_GPIO_CTRL1,
+			   RT5645_I2S2_DAC_PIN_MASK, RT5645_I2S2_DAC_PIN_GPIO);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
